### lem-the-diagonal-map-is-an-embedding
38:## Statement
75:## Proof
79:1.1 Suppose $f$ and $g$ are continuous; then the components $\pi_0 \circ \langle f, g \rangle = f$ and $\pi_1 \circ \langle f, g \rangle = g$ of $\langle f, g \rangle$ are continuous, so $\langle f, g \rangle$ is continuous. [A1, L1]
81:1.2 Suppose $\langle f, g \rangle$ is continuous; then its components $f$ and $g$ are continuous. [A1, L1]
83:1.3 $\delta_X$ is continuous, its two components both being $\mathrm{id}_X$, which is continuous. [A1, L1, L2]
85:1.4 $\delta_X$ is injective: if $\delta_X(x) = \delta_X(x')$ then reading the coordinate at $0$ gives $x = x'$. [A1]
87:1.5 The image of $\delta_X$ is $\Delta_X$: each $(x,x)$ lies in $\Delta_X$, and each $z \in \Delta_X$ satisfies $z = (z_0, z_0) = \delta_X(z_0)$. [A1]
89:1.6 The restriction $p := \pi_0|_{\Delta_X} : \Delta_X \to X$ is continuous, being the restriction of the continuous $\pi_0$ to a subspace; and $\pi_0|_{\Delta_X} = \pi_1|_{\Delta_X}$, since $z_0 = z_1$ for $z \in \Delta_X$. [A1, L1, L3]
91:2.1 Steps 1.1 and 1.2 together are claim 1. [step 1.1, step 1.2]
93:2.2 The corestriction $\delta_X^{0} : X \to \Delta_X$ is continuous, since composing it with the inclusion $\Delta_X \to X \times X$ gives $\delta_X$, which is continuous by step 1.3. [step 1.3, L3]
95:2.3 $\delta_X^{0}$ and $p$ are mutually inverse: $p(\delta_X^{0}(x)) = \pi_0(x,x) = x$ for $x \in X$, and $\delta_X^{0}(p(z)) = (z_0, z_0) = (z_0, z_1) = z$ for $z \in \Delta_X$, the middle equality holding because $z_0 = z_1$. [step 1.5, step 1.6, A1]
97:3.1 By steps 2.2, 2.3 and 1.6 the map $\delta_X^{0}$ is a continuous bijection with continuous inverse $p$, hence a homeomorphism, and its inverse is $\pi_0|_{\Delta_X} = \pi_1|_{\Delta_X}$; this is claim 3 and, with steps 1.4 and 1.5, claim 2. [step 1.4, step 1.5, step 1.6, step 2.2, step 2.3, L4]
99:4.1 Claims 1, 2 and 3 are steps 2.1, 3.1 and 3.1 respectively, so the lemma is proved. [step 2.1, step 3.1] ∎
### thm-hausdorff-iff-the-diagonal-is-closed
39:## Statement
65:## Proof
69:1.1 Assume $X$ is Hausdorff and let $z \in X \times X$ with $z \notin \Delta_X$, so that $z_0 \ne z_1$; by [A1] there are open $U \ni z_0$ and $V \ni z_1$ with $U \cap V = \varnothing$. [A1]
71:1.2 Assume $\Delta_X$ is closed and let $x, y \in X$ with $x \ne y$; then $z := (x,y)$ satisfies $z \notin \Delta_X = \overline{\Delta_X}$, the equality holding by [L1] since $\Delta_X$ is closed. [L1]
73:2.1 The box $U \times V$ of step 1.1 is a basic open set containing $z$, and $(U \times V) \cap \Delta_X = \varnothing$: a point $w$ of the intersection would satisfy $w_0 = w_1$ with $w_0 \in U$ and $w_1 \in V$, putting $w_0$ in $U \cap V = \varnothing$. [step 1.1, A2]
75:2.2 By [L1] applied to the basis of [A2], step 1.2 supplies a basic open box $U \times V$ with $z \in U \times V$ and $(U \times V) \cap \Delta_X = \varnothing$; so $x \in U$ and $y \in V$. [step 1.2, A2, L1]
77:3.1 From step 2.1 and [L1], $z \notin \overline{\Delta_X}$ for every $z \notin \Delta_X$; hence $\overline{\Delta_X} \subseteq \Delta_X$, and with [L2] this gives $\overline{\Delta_X} = \Delta_X$, so $\Delta_X$ is closed. [step 1.1, step 2.1, L1, L2]
79:3.2 The sets $U$ and $V$ of step 2.2 are disjoint: if $t \in U \cap V$ then $(t,t)$ lies in $U \times V$ and in $\Delta_X$, contradicting $(U \times V) \cap \Delta_X = \varnothing$. [step 2.2]
81:4.1 Step 3.1 shows that $X$ Hausdorff implies $\Delta_X$ closed, and steps 2.2 and 3.2 show that $\Delta_X$ closed implies that any two distinct points of $X$ have disjoint open neighbourhoods, which by [A1] is the Hausdorff condition; the two implications are the theorem. [step 2.2, step 3.1, step 3.2, A1] ∎
### thm-the-closed-graph-criterion-over-a-compact-hausdorff-codomain
41:## Statement
85:## Proof
89:1.1 Assume $Y$ is compact and $G_f$ is closed, and put $N := (X \times Y) \setminus G_f$, which is open. [A3]
91:1.2 Fix $x_0 \in X$ and an open $V \subseteq Y$ with $f(x_0) \in V$, and put $C := Y \setminus V$; then $C$ is closed in $Y$, hence a compact subspace. [A3, L2]
93:1.3 Let $\mathcal{P}$ be the set of all pairs $(U, W)$ such that $U$ is open in $X$, $W$ is open in $Y$, $x_0 \in U$ and $(U \times W) \cap G_f = \varnothing$; this family is specified by a formula and nothing is selected in forming it. [construct]
95:1.4 If $Y$ is Hausdorff and $f$ is continuous then $G_f$ is closed, which is claim 2. [L5]
97:2.1 Every $y \in C$ lies in $W$ for some $(U,W) \in \mathcal{P}$: since $f(x_0) \in V$ and $y \notin V$ we have $y \ne f(x_0)$, so $(x_0, y) \in N$, and by [A1] there is a basic box $U \times W$ with $(x_0,y) \in U \times W \subseteq N$, which gives $(U,W) \in \mathcal{P}$. [step 1.1, step 1.2, step 1.3, A1]
99:3.1 The family $\mathcal{V} := \{\, W \cap C : (U,W) \in \mathcal{P} \text{ for some } U \,\}$ consists of sets open in the subspace $C$ and its union is $C$, by step 2.1. [step 2.1, L1]
101:4.1 By compactness of $C$ there is a finite subfamily of $\mathcal{V}$ whose union is $C$; being finite it may be listed as $V_0, \dots, V_{n-1}$ for some $n \in \mathbb{N}$, so that $C \subseteq \bigcup_{i<n} V_i$. [step 1.2, step 3.1, L1, L3]
103:5.1 For each $i < n$ the set $\mathcal{P}_i := \{\, (U,W) \in \mathcal{P} : W \cap C = V_i \,\}$ is nonempty, since $V_i \in \mathcal{V}$; so by [L4] applied to the function $i \mapsto \mathcal{P}_i$ on $n$ there is a choice function on the family of these sets, and it supplies a pair $(U_i, W_i) \in \mathcal{P}_i$ for every $i < n$. [step 4.1, L4, choose]
105:6.1 Put $U := \{\, x \in X : x \in U_i \text{ for every } i < n \,\}$; this is $X$ when $n = 0$ and a finite intersection of open sets otherwise, hence open in either case, and $x_0 \in U$ since $x_0 \in U_i$ for every $i < n$. [step 5.1, A3, construct]
107:7.1 $f[U] \subseteq V$: let $x \in U$ and suppose $f(x) \notin V$, that is $f(x) \in C$; then $f(x) \in V_i = W_i \cap C \subseteq W_i$ for some $i < n$ by step 4.1, while $x \in U \subseteq U_i$, so the point $(x, f(x))$ of $G_f$ lies in $U_i \times W_i$, contradicting $(U_i \times W_i) \cap G_f = \varnothing$; hence $f(x) \in V$. [step 4.1, step 5.1, step 6.1]
109:8.1 By steps 6.1 and 7.1 there is, for the arbitrary $x_0 \in X$ and the arbitrary open $V$ containing $f(x_0)$ fixed in step 1.2, an open $U \ni x_0$ with $f[U] \subseteq V$; so $f$ is continuous by [A2], which is claim 1. [step 1.2, step 6.1, step 7.1, A2]
111:9.1 If $Y$ is compact and Hausdorff then step 8.1 gives one implication and step 1.4 the other, so continuity of $f$ and closedness of $G_f$ are equivalent, which is claim 3; with steps 8.1 and 1.4 the theorem is proved. [step 8.1, step 1.4] ∎
### cor-contractible-iff-identity-nullhomotopic
22:## Statement
37:## Proof
41:1.1 If $X$ is contractible, apply [A1] to the continuous map $\operatorname{id}_X:X\to X$ to conclude that $\operatorname{id}_X$ is nullhomotopic. [A1]
43:1.2 Conversely suppose $\operatorname{id}_X\simeq c_{x_0}$ for some $x_0\in X$, and let $f:X\to Y$ be any continuous map. Postcomposition by $f$ gives $f=f\circ\operatorname{id}_X\simeq f\circ c_{x_0}=c_{f(x_0)}$ by [L1]. Thus $f$ is nullhomotopic. [assume-hyp, L1, A1]
45:2.1 Since $Y$ and $f$ in step 1.2 were arbitrary, every continuous map out of $X$ is nullhomotopic, so $X$ is contractible by [A1]. Together with step 1.1 this proves the equivalence. [step 1.1, step 1.2, A1] ∎

