ex-the-graph-of-a-continuous-real-function-is-closed L2 For $f : \mathbb{R} \to \mathbb{R}$, continuity in the sense of [[def-continuity-real]] is continuity as a map of metric spaces ([[lem-real-and-metric-notions-agree]], claim 1), and continuity as a map of metric spaces is continuity as a map of topological spaces for the metric topologies ([[def-metrizable-space]], [[def-continuous-map-top]]).
cex-a-closed-graph-without-continuity L2 The reciprocal $x \mapsto 1/x$ is continuous at every $c \ne 0$ as a function on $\{\, x \in \mathbb{R} : x \ne 0 \,\}$ ([[thm-algebra-of-continuous-functions]], claims 4 and 5), continuity being the $\varepsilon$-$\delta$ condition of [[def-continuity-real]]; and for a real function that condition at a point is continuity at that point as a map of topological spaces ([[lem-real-and-metric-notions-agree]], claim 1, [[def-metrizable-space]], [[def-continuous-map-top]]).
ex-two-continuous-real-functions-agreeing-on-the-rationals-are-equal L3 For a real function, $\varepsilon$-$\delta$ continuity is continuity as a map of topological spaces ([[lem-real-and-metric-notions-agree]], claim 1, [[def-metrizable-space]], [[def-continuous-map-top]]).
cex-two-maps-into-an-indiscrete-space-agreeing-on-a-dense-set L1 A function between topological spaces is continuous exactly when the preimage of every open set is open ([[thm-continuity-characterisations-top]], clauses (a) and (b), [[def-continuous-map-top]]).

### def-hausdorff-space
thm-hausdorff-iff-the-diagonal-is-closed A1 $X$ is Hausdorff when for all $x \ne y$ in $X$ there are open $U \ni x$ and $V \ni y$ with $U \cap V = \varnothing$ ([[def-hausdorff-space]]).
cor-the-agreement-set-of-two-maps-into-a-hausdorff-space-is-closed L2 $\Delta_Y$ is closed in $Y \times Y$, since $Y$ is Hausdorff ([[thm-hausdorff-iff-the-diagonal-is-closed]], [[def-hausdorff-space]]).
cor-two-continuous-maps-into-a-hausdorff-space-agreeing-on-a-dense-set-are-equal L1 The agreement set $E(f,g) = \{\, z \in Z : f(z) = g(z) \,\}$ is closed in $Z$ ([[cor-the-agreement-set-of-two-maps-into-a-hausdorff-space-is-closed]], [[def-hausdorff-space]], [[def-continuous-map-top]]).
lem-a-sequence-in-a-hausdorff-space-has-at-most-one-limit A1 $X$ is Hausdorff: distinct points have disjoint open neighbourhoods ([[def-hausdorff-space]]).
thm-a-compact-hausdorff-space-is-regular-and-normal L2 In a Hausdorff space, a point and a disjoint compact set have disjoint open neighbourhoods, and two disjoint compact sets have disjoint open neighbourhoods ([[thm-compact-subset-of-a-hausdorff-space-is-closed]], [[def-hausdorff-space]], [[def-compact-space]]).
lem-the-graph-of-a-continuous-map-into-a-hausdorff-space-is-closed L3 If $Y$ is Hausdorff and $u, v : Z \to Y$ are continuous, then $\{\, z \in Z : u(z) = v(z) \,\}$ is closed in $Z$ ([[cor-the-agreement-set-of-two-maps-into-a-hausdorff-space-is-closed]], [[def-hausdorff-space]]).
thm-the-closed-graph-criterion-over-a-compact-hausdorff-codomain L5 If $Y$ is Hausdorff and $f$ is continuous then $G_f$ is closed in $X \times Y$ ([[lem-the-graph-of-a-continuous-map-into-a-hausdorff-space-is-closed]], [[def-hausdorff-space]]).
lem-a-locally-compact-hausdorff-space-has-a-base-of-open-sets-with-compact-closure A2 $X$ is Hausdorff: distinct points have disjoint open neighbourhoods ([[def-hausdorff-space]]).
fs-two-continuous-maps-agreeing-on-a-dense-subset-are-equal A2 The indiscrete topology on $Y_0$ is $\{\varnothing, Y_0\}$; the two-point indiscrete space is not Hausdorff, the only open set containing either point being $Y_0$ ([[def-standard-topologies]], [[def-hausdorff-space]], [[def-topological-space]]).
ex-the-diagonal-in-the-cofinite-and-cocountable-topologies L3 A space is Hausdorff exactly when its diagonal is closed in its square ([[thm-hausdorff-iff-the-diagonal-is-closed]], [[def-hausdorff-space]]).
ex-the-graph-of-a-continuous-real-function-is-closed L1 $\mathbb{R}$ with its usual topology is Hausdorff, being metrizable ([[def-metrizable-space]], [[def-hausdorff-space]]).
cex-a-closed-graph-without-continuity A1 $\mathbb{R}$ with its usual topology is metrizable and hence Hausdorff ([[lem-real-line-is-a-metric-space]], [[def-metrizable-space]], [[def-hausdorff-space]]); $\mathbb{R}^2$ carries the product topology ([[def-product-topology]], [[lem-product-topology-on-rn]]).
ex-two-continuous-real-functions-agreeing-on-the-rationals-are-equal L2 $\mathbb{R}$ with its usual topology is Hausdorff, being metrizable ([[def-metrizable-space]], [[def-hausdorff-space]]).
cex-two-maps-into-an-indiscrete-space-agreeing-on-a-dense-set A2 The indiscrete topology on $Y_0$ is $\{\varnothing, Y_0\}$, and the two-point indiscrete space is not Hausdorff ([[def-standard-topologies]], [[def-hausdorff-space]]).
ex-separating-a-point-from-a-finite-set-in-a-hausdorff-space L2 In a Hausdorff space a point and a disjoint compact set have disjoint open neighbourhoods, two disjoint compact sets have disjoint open neighbourhoods, and every compact subset is closed ([[thm-compact-subset-of-a-hausdorff-space-is-closed]], [[def-hausdorff-space]]).

### def-homeomorphism-and-open-maps
lem-the-diagonal-map-is-an-embedding L4 A continuous bijection whose inverse is continuous is a homeomorphism, and a map that is injective and restricts to a homeomorphism onto its image with the subspace topology is an embedding ([[def-homeomorphism-and-open-maps]]).
fs-homotopy-equivalent-spaces-are-homeomorphic L2 A homeomorphism is in particular a bijection ([[def-homeomorphism-and-open-maps]]).
ex-a-point-and-r-are-homotopy-equivalent-not-homeomorphic L3 A homeomorphism is a bijection, and $\mathbb R$ is uncountable ([[def-homeomorphism-and-open-maps]], [[thm-r-uncountable]]).

### def-homotopy-relative-and-path-homotopy
lem-homotopy-reflexive-and-symmetric A1 A homotopy rel $A$ is a continuous $K:X\times I\to Y$ with $K(x,0)$ and $K(x,1)$ the prescribed endpoint maps and $K(a,t)$ equal to their common value for every $a\in A$ and $t\in I$ ([[def-homotopy-relative-and-path-homotopy]]).
lem-homotopy-transitivity-by-reparametrisation A1 The endpoint and relative conditions for $F$ and $G$ are those of [[def-homotopy-relative-and-path-homotopy]].
thm-composition-respects-homotopy A1 The endpoint and relative conditions are those of [[def-homotopy-relative-and-path-homotopy]].
thm-two-maps-into-a-convex-set-are-homotopic A1 A homotopy from $f$ to $g$ is a continuous $H:X\times I\to C$ with $H(x,0)=f(x)$ and $H(x,1)=g(x)$ ([[def-homotopy-relative-and-path-homotopy]]).
ex-path-homotopy-through-straight-segments-in-a-convex-set A1 A path homotopy relative endpoints is a homotopy $H:I\times I\to C$ with the path parameter on the first coordinate and with $H(0,t)$ and $H(1,t)$ fixed ([[def-homotopy-relative-and-path-homotopy]]).

### def-interval
fs-a-function-with-closed-graph-is-continuous A1 A set $U \subseteq \mathbb{R}$ is open in the usual topology exactly when for every $x \in U$ there is a real $r > 0$ with $(x - r, x + r) \subseteq U$; in particular every bounded open interval is open ([[lem-real-line-is-a-metric-space]], claims 2 and 3, [[def-metrizable-space]], [[def-interval]]).
fs-two-continuous-maps-agreeing-on-a-dense-subset-are-equal A1 $U \subseteq \mathbb{R}$ is open in the usual topology exactly when for every $x \in U$ there is a real $r > 0$ with $(x-r, x+r) \subseteq U$ ([[lem-real-line-is-a-metric-space]], claims 2 and 3, [[def-metrizable-space]], [[def-interval]]).
ex-the-diagonal-of-the-real-line-is-closed A1 Every bounded open interval is open in $\mathbb{R}$ ([[lem-real-line-is-a-metric-space]], claims 2 and 3, [[def-metrizable-space]], [[def-interval]]).
ex-two-continuous-real-functions-agreeing-on-the-rationals-are-equal A1 $U \subseteq \mathbb{R}$ is open in the usual topology exactly when for every $x \in U$ there is a real $r > 0$ with $(x - r, x + r) \subseteq U$ ([[lem-real-line-is-a-metric-space]], claims 2 and 3, [[def-metrizable-space]], [[def-interval]]).
cex-two-maps-into-an-indiscrete-space-agreeing-on-a-dense-set A1 $U \subseteq \mathbb{R}$ is open in the usual topology exactly when for every $x \in U$ there is a real $r > 0$ with $(x - r, x + r) \subseteq U$ ([[lem-real-line-is-a-metric-space]], claims 2 and 3, [[def-metrizable-space]], [[def-interval]]).
ex-intervals-and-euclidean-spaces-are-contractible A1 Intervals are order-convex: if $x,c\in J$ and $x\le z\le c$, or $c\le z\le x$, then $z\in J$ ([[def-interval]]).

### def-path-connected
cor-homotopy-equivalence-bijection-on-path-components A1 Path components are the equivalence classes for the relation “joined by a path” ([[def-path-connected]]).
cor-contractible-spaces-are-path-connected A1 Paths define an equivalence relation: paths may be reversed and concatenated, and $X$ is path-connected exactly when every pair of points is joined by a path ([[def-path-connected]]).
fs-a-retract-must-be-a-deformation-retract L2 A nonempty space whose identity is nullhomotopic is contractible, and every nonempty contractible space is path-connected ([[cor-contractible-iff-identity-nullhomotopic]], [[cor-contractible-spaces-are-path-connected]], [[def-path-connected]]).
ex-a-two-point-retract-that-is-not-a-deformation-retract L2 The refutation in [[fs-a-retract-must-be-a-deformation-retract]] proves that this $A$ is a retract of $X$ but not a deformation retract, because such a deformation would force the disconnected space $X$ to be path-connected ([[def-path-connected]]).

### def-product-topology
thm-hausdorff-iff-the-diagonal-is-closed A2 The boxes $U \times V$ with $U, V \in \mathcal{T}$ form a basis for the product topology on $X \times X$, the index set being $2$ ([[def-product-topology]], [[def-topology-basis-subbasis]], [[def-the-diagonal-of-a-space]]).
lem-the-graph-of-a-continuous-map-into-a-hausdorff-space-is-closed A1 $G_f = \{\, z \in X \times Y : (f \circ \pi_0)(z) = \pi_1(z) \,\}$, since $(f \circ \pi_0)(z) = f(z_0)$ and $\pi_1(z) = z_1$ ([[def-product-topology]], [[def-the-diagonal-of-a-space]]).
thm-the-closed-graph-criterion-over-a-compact-hausdorff-codomain A1 The boxes $U \times W$ with $U$ open in $X$ and $W$ open in $Y$ form a basis for the product topology on $X \times Y$, the index set being $2$ ([[def-product-topology]], [[def-topology-basis-subbasis]]).
fs-a-function-with-closed-graph-is-continuous A2 The boxes $U \times W$ with $U$ and $W$ open in $\mathbb{R}$ form a basis for the product topology on $\mathbb{R} \times \mathbb{R}$, the index set being $2$ ([[def-product-topology]], [[def-topology-basis-subbasis]]).
ex-the-diagonal-of-the-real-line-is-closed A2 The boxes $U \times W$ with $U$ and $W$ open in $\mathbb{R}$ form a basis for the product topology on $\mathbb{R}^2$, the index set being $2$ ([[def-product-topology]], [[def-topology-basis-subbasis]], [[lem-product-topology-on-rn]]).
ex-the-diagonal-in-the-cofinite-and-cocountable-topologies A2 The boxes $U \times W$ with $U$ and $W$ open form a basis for the product topology on a square, the index set being $2$ ([[def-product-topology]], [[def-topology-basis-subbasis]]).
cex-a-closed-graph-without-continuity A1 $\mathbb{R}$ with its usual topology is metrizable and hence Hausdorff ([[lem-real-line-is-a-metric-space]], [[def-metrizable-space]], [[def-hausdorff-space]]); $\mathbb{R}^2$ carries the product topology ([[def-product-topology]], [[lem-product-topology-on-rn]]).
ex-a-finite-hausdorff-space-is-discrete A3 The boxes $U \times W$ with $U, W \in \mathcal{T}$ form a basis for the product topology on $X \times X$, the index set being $2$ ([[def-product-topology]], [[def-topology-basis-subbasis]], [[def-the-diagonal-of-a-space]]).
ex-a-cylinder-deformation-retracts-onto-an-end L1 Product projections are continuous, and a map into a product is continuous exactly when its components are continuous ([[thm-product-universal-property]], [[def-product-topology]]).

### def-subspace-topology-top
lem-the-diagonal-map-is-an-embedding L3 For $S \subseteq W$ with the subspace topology, a function $g_0 : Z \to S$ is continuous if and only if $\iota \circ g_0 : Z \to W$ is continuous, $\iota$ being the inclusion; and the restriction of a continuous map $W \to Y$ to $S$ is continuous ([[def-subspace-topology-top]]).
thm-the-closed-graph-criterion-over-a-compact-hausdorff-codomain L1 A space is compact when every family of its open sets whose union is the whole space has a finite subfamily whose union is the whole space; a subset is compact when it is compact as a subspace, whose open sets are the traces of the open sets of the ambient space ([[def-compact-space]], [[def-subspace-topology-top]]).
lem-a-locally-compact-hausdorff-space-has-a-base-of-open-sets-with-compact-closure L1 For $S \subseteq X$ the open sets of the subspace $S$ are the traces $U' \cap S$ of the open sets of $X$; an open subset of $X$ contained in $S$ is open in $S$; and for $S \subseteq T \subseteq X$ the topology $S$ inherits from $T$ is the topology it inherits from $X$ ([[def-subspace-topology-top]]).
ex-separating-a-point-from-a-finite-set-in-a-hausdorff-space A2 A space is compact when every family of its open sets whose union is the whole space has a finite subfamily whose union is the whole space; a subset is compact when it is compact as a subspace ([[def-compact-space]], [[def-subspace-topology-top]]).
lem-homotopy-reflexive-and-symmetric L3 In the usual topology of $\mathbb R$, the open balls are the intervals $(u-r,u+r)$; $I=[0,1]$ has the subspace topology ([[lem-real-line-is-a-metric-space]], [[def-subspace-topology-top]]).
lem-homotopy-transitivity-by-reparametrisation L2 In a subspace, closed sets are exactly traces of ambient closed sets; restrictions of continuous maps to subspaces are continuous ([[def-subspace-topology-top]]).
lem-homotopy-transitivity-by-reparametrisation L5 In the usual topology of $\mathbb R$, open balls are open intervals; $I=[0,1]$ has the subspace topology ([[lem-real-line-is-a-metric-space]], [[def-subspace-topology-top]]).
lem-straight-line-homotopies-are-continuous L4 A map into a subspace is continuous exactly when its composite with the ambient inclusion is continuous ([[def-subspace-topology-top]]).

### def-t0-and-t1-spaces
thm-a-compact-hausdorff-space-is-regular-and-normal A1 $X$ is regular when for every closed $C \subseteq X$ and every $x \in X \setminus C$ there are disjoint open $U \ni x$ and $V \supseteq C$; the case $C = \varnothing$ is met by $U = X$ and $V = \varnothing$, and $T_3$ is regular together with $T_1$ ([[def-regular-and-t3-spaces]], [[def-t0-and-t1-spaces]]).
thm-a-compact-hausdorff-space-is-regular-and-normal A2 $X$ is normal when for all disjoint closed $A, B \subseteq X$ there are disjoint open $U \supseteq A$ and $V \supseteq B$; the cases $A = \varnothing$ and $B = \varnothing$ are met by $\varnothing$ together with $X$, and $T_4$ is normal together with $T_1$ ([[def-normal-and-t4-spaces]], [[def-t0-and-t1-spaces]]).
thm-a-compact-hausdorff-space-is-regular-and-normal L3 Every Hausdorff space is $T_1$ ([[lem-urysohn-sits-between-hausdorff-and-regular-plus-t1]], claim 2, [[def-t0-and-t1-spaces]]).
ex-the-diagonal-in-the-cofinite-and-cocountable-topologies L1 A space is $T_1$ exactly when every singleton is closed ([[thm-t1-iff-singletons-are-closed]], clause (b), [[def-t0-and-t1-spaces]]).
ex-a-finite-hausdorff-space-is-discrete L1 Every Hausdorff space is $T_1$ ([[lem-urysohn-sits-between-hausdorff-and-regular-plus-t1]], claim 2, [[def-t0-and-t1-spaces]]).

### lem-continuity-is-local-and-pastes
lem-the-graph-of-a-continuous-map-into-a-hausdorff-space-is-closed L2 A composite of continuous maps is continuous ([[lem-continuity-is-local-and-pastes]], claim 1).

### lem-real-line-is-a-metric-space
fs-a-function-with-closed-graph-is-continuous A1 A set $U \subseteq \mathbb{R}$ is open in the usual topology exactly when for every $x \in U$ there is a real $r > 0$ with $(x - r, x + r) \subseteq U$; in particular every bounded open interval is open ([[lem-real-line-is-a-metric-space]], claims 2 and 3, [[def-metrizable-space]], [[def-interval]]).
fs-two-continuous-maps-agreeing-on-a-dense-subset-are-equal A1 $U \subseteq \mathbb{R}$ is open in the usual topology exactly when for every $x \in U$ there is a real $r > 0$ with $(x-r, x+r) \subseteq U$ ([[lem-real-line-is-a-metric-space]], claims 2 and 3, [[def-metrizable-space]], [[def-interval]]).
ex-the-diagonal-of-the-real-line-is-closed A1 Every bounded open interval is open in $\mathbb{R}$ ([[lem-real-line-is-a-metric-space]], claims 2 and 3, [[def-metrizable-space]], [[def-interval]]).
ex-the-graph-of-a-continuous-real-function-is-closed A1 The usual topology of $\mathbb{R}$ is the metric topology of $d_{\mathbb{R}}(x,y) = |x-y|$ ([[lem-real-line-is-a-metric-space]], claim 3, [[def-metrizable-space]]).
cex-a-closed-graph-without-continuity A1 $\mathbb{R}$ with its usual topology is metrizable and hence Hausdorff ([[lem-real-line-is-a-metric-space]], [[def-metrizable-space]], [[def-hausdorff-space]]); $\mathbb{R}^2$ carries the product topology ([[def-product-topology]], [[lem-product-topology-on-rn]]).
ex-two-continuous-real-functions-agreeing-on-the-rationals-are-equal A1 $U \subseteq \mathbb{R}$ is open in the usual topology exactly when for every $x \in U$ there is a real $r > 0$ with $(x - r, x + r) \subseteq U$ ([[lem-real-line-is-a-metric-space]], claims 2 and 3, [[def-metrizable-space]], [[def-interval]]).
cex-two-maps-into-an-indiscrete-space-agreeing-on-a-dense-set A1 $U \subseteq \mathbb{R}$ is open in the usual topology exactly when for every $x \in U$ there is a real $r > 0$ with $(x - r, x + r) \subseteq U$ ([[lem-real-line-is-a-metric-space]], claims 2 and 3, [[def-metrizable-space]], [[def-interval]]).
lem-homotopy-reflexive-and-symmetric L3 In the usual topology of $\mathbb R$, the open balls are the intervals $(u-r,u+r)$; $I=[0,1]$ has the subspace topology ([[lem-real-line-is-a-metric-space]], [[def-subspace-topology-top]]).
lem-homotopy-transitivity-by-reparametrisation L5 In the usual topology of $\mathbb R$, open balls are open intervals; $I=[0,1]$ has the subspace topology ([[lem-real-line-is-a-metric-space]], [[def-subspace-topology-top]]).

### thm-closure-characterisation-top
thm-hausdorff-iff-the-diagonal-is-closed L1 For a basis $\mathcal{B}$ of a space, a point lies in $\overline{A}$ if and only if every $B \in \mathcal{B}$ containing it meets $A$; and $A$ is closed if and only if $A = \overline{A}$ ([[thm-closure-characterisation-top]], claims 1(d) and 2, [[def-interior-closure-boundary-top]]).
cor-two-continuous-maps-into-a-hausdorff-space-agreeing-on-a-dense-set-are-equal L2 $\overline{A}$ is the smallest closed superset of $A$: it is contained in every closed set containing $A$ ([[thm-closure-characterisation-top]], claim 2, [[def-interior-closure-boundary-top]]).
fs-a-function-with-closed-graph-is-continuous L1 A point lies in $\overline{A}$ exactly when every basic open set containing it meets $A$, and $A$ is closed exactly when $A = \overline{A}$ ([[thm-closure-characterisation-top]], claims 1(d) and 2).
ex-the-diagonal-of-the-real-line-is-closed L1 A point lies in $\overline{A}$ exactly when every basic open set containing it meets $A$, and $A$ is closed exactly when $A = \overline{A}$ ([[thm-closure-characterisation-top]], claims 1(d) and 2).
ex-the-diagonal-in-the-cofinite-and-cocountable-topologies L2 A point lies in $\overline{A}$ exactly when every basic open set containing it meets $A$, and $A$ is closed exactly when $A = \overline{A}$ ([[thm-closure-characterisation-top]], claims 1(d) and 2).
cex-two-maps-into-an-indiscrete-space-agreeing-on-a-dense-set L4 A set is closed exactly when it equals its own closure ([[thm-closure-characterisation-top]], claim 2).

### thm-continuity-characterisations-top
cor-the-agreement-set-of-two-maps-into-a-hausdorff-space-is-closed L3 A map is continuous if and only if the preimage of every closed set is closed ([[thm-continuity-characterisations-top]], clauses (a) and (c), [[def-continuous-map-top]]).
fs-two-continuous-maps-agreeing-on-a-dense-subset-are-equal L1 A function between topological spaces is continuous exactly when the preimage of every open set is open ([[thm-continuity-characterisations-top]], clauses (a) and (b), [[def-continuous-map-top]]).
cex-two-maps-into-an-indiscrete-space-agreeing-on-a-dense-set L1 A function between topological spaces is continuous exactly when the preimage of every open set is open ([[thm-continuity-characterisations-top]], clauses (a) and (b), [[def-continuous-map-top]]).
lem-homotopy-reflexive-and-symmetric L2 A map is continuous exactly when preimages of open sets are open ([[thm-continuity-characterisations-top]], condition (b)).
lem-homotopy-transitivity-by-reparametrisation L1 A map is continuous exactly when preimages of closed sets are closed ([[thm-continuity-characterisations-top]], condition (c)).
thm-composition-respects-homotopy L2 A map is continuous exactly when preimages of open sets are open ([[thm-continuity-characterisations-top]], condition (b)).
cor-homotopy-equivalence-bijection-on-path-components L2 A map is continuous exactly when preimages of open sets are open ([[thm-continuity-characterisations-top]]).
lem-straight-line-homotopies-are-continuous L5 A map is continuous exactly when preimages of open sets are open ([[thm-continuity-characterisations-top]], condition (b)).
cor-contractible-spaces-are-path-connected L3 A map is continuous exactly when preimages of open sets are open ([[thm-continuity-characterisations-top]]).
ex-a-cylinder-deformation-retracts-onto-an-end L3 A map is continuous exactly when preimages of open sets are open ([[thm-continuity-characterisations-top]]).

