46:2.1 Apply [L1] to step 1.1 and the point $c$ to obtain the stated contraction of $J$, and apply it to step 1.2 and any chosen point of $\mathbb R^n$ to obtain the Euclidean contraction. [step 1.1, step 1.2, L1] ∎
### ex-a-point-and-r-are-homotopy-equivalent-not-homeomorphic
42:1.1 Both $i$ and $q$ are continuous, since the preimage of any open set is either empty or the entire one-point source or target preimage. [construct]
44:1.2 No bijection exists from the finite set $P$ to the uncountable set $\mathbb R$, so no homeomorphism exists by [L3]. [L3]
46:2.1 One has $q\circ i=\operatorname{id}_P$ and $i\circ q=c_0\simeq\operatorname{id}_{\mathbb R}$ by [L1]. Hence $i$ and $q$ are homotopy inverses by [L2]. [step 1.1, L1, L2]
48:3.1 Steps 2.1 and 1.2 verify the claimed contrast, agreeing with [L4]. [step 2.1, step 1.2, L4] ∎
### ex-a-two-point-retract-that-is-not-a-deformation-retract
40:1.1 The map $r(0)=r(1)=0$ is continuous by [L1] and fixes the point of $A$, so it is a retraction. [L1, A1]
42:1.2 If a deformation retraction existed, [A1] would make $\operatorname{id}_X$ homotopic to the constant map at $0$; the full contradiction with the separation $X=\{0\}\cup\{1\}$ is established in [L2]. [assume-hyp, A1, L2]
44:2.1 Therefore $A$ is a retract but not a deformation retract of $X$. [step 1.1, step 1.2] ∎
### ex-a-cylinder-deformation-retracts-onto-an-end
46:1.1 The map $r:Z\to A$, $r(x,t)=(x,0)$, is continuous: its composite with the inclusion $A\hookrightarrow Z$ has the continuous components $(x,t)\mapsto x$ and the constant $0$, so [L1] applies. It fixes every $(x,0)\in A$, hence is a retraction. [L1, A1]
48:1.2 On the source $Z$, the second projection $p_I:Z\to I$ and the constant zero map are continuous. Since $I$ is convex, [L2] makes $L:Z\times I\to I$, $L((x,t),s)=(1-s)t$, continuous. [L1, L2]
50:1.3 One has $K((x,t),0)=(x,t)$, $K((x,t),1)=(x,0)$, and $K((x,0),s)=(x,0)$ for every $s\in I$. [algebra]
52:2.1 The first component $((x,t),s)\mapsto x$ is continuous as a composite of product projections, since the preimage of an open set is an iterated preimage and hence open by [L3]. Together with step 1.2, [L1] makes $K((x,t),s)=(x,L((x,t),s))$ continuous into $Z$. [step 1.2, L1, L3]
54:3.1 Steps 1.1, 2.1 and 1.3 satisfy [A1], so $(r,K)$ is a deformation retraction of $Z$ onto $A$. [step 1.1, step 2.1, step 1.3, A1] ∎

