items/lem-a-locally-compact-hausdorff-space-has-a-base-of-open-sets-with-compact-closure.md:96:1.3 $K$ is closed in $X$, being a compact subset of the Hausdorff space $X$. [A2, L2]
items/lem-a-locally-compact-hausdorff-space-has-a-base-of-open-sets-with-compact-closure.md:98:2.1 The subspace $K$ is compact and Hausdorff, hence regular. [step 1.2, L3]
items/lem-a-locally-compact-hausdorff-space-has-a-base-of-open-sets-with-compact-closure.md:100:2.2 Put $G := U \cap \operatorname{int}(K)$; it is open in $X$, contains $x$ by step 1.1, is contained in $K$ by [A3], and is therefore also open in the subspace $K$. [step 1.1, A3, L1]
items/lem-a-locally-compact-hausdorff-space-has-a-base-of-open-sets-with-compact-closure.md:102:3.1 Applying [L4] inside the space $K$, which is regular by step 2.1, to the point $x$ and the set $G$ open in $K$, there is a set $V$ open in $K$ with $x \in V \subseteq \operatorname{cl}_K(V) \subseteq G$. [step 2.1, step 2.2, L4, choose]
items/lem-a-locally-compact-hausdorff-space-has-a-base-of-open-sets-with-compact-closure.md:104:4.1 $V$ is open in $X$: by [L1] there is an open $V' \subseteq X$ with $V = V' \cap K$, and since $V \subseteq G \subseteq \operatorname{int}(K)$ we get $V = V \cap \operatorname{int}(K) = V' \cap K \cap \operatorname{int}(K) = V' \cap \operatorname{int}(K)$, an intersection of two open subsets of $X$. [step 2.2, step 3.1, L1, A3]
items/lem-a-locally-compact-hausdorff-space-has-a-base-of-open-sets-with-compact-closure.md:106:4.2 $\overline{V} = \operatorname{cl}_K(V)$: from $V \subseteq K$ and $K$ closed in $X$ (step 1.3) the smallest closed superset of $V$ satisfies $\overline{V} \subseteq K$, and [L5] gives $\operatorname{cl}_K(V) = \overline{V} \cap K = \overline{V}$. [step 1.3, step 3.1, A3, L5]
items/lem-a-locally-compact-hausdorff-space-has-a-base-of-open-sets-with-compact-closure.md:108:5.1 $\overline{V}$ is compact: by step 4.2 it is $\operatorname{cl}_K(V)$, which is closed in the compact subspace $K$ and hence compact by [L6]; and by the transitivity clause of [L1] the topology it inherits from $K$ is the one it inherits from $X$, so it is a compact subset of $X$. [step 4.2, L1, L6]
items/lem-a-locally-compact-hausdorff-space-has-a-base-of-open-sets-with-compact-closure.md:110:6.1 Combining, $V$ is open in $X$ by step 4.1, $x \in V \subseteq \overline{V} = \operatorname{cl}_K(V) \subseteq G \subseteq U$ by steps 3.1, 4.2 and 2.2, and $\overline{V}$ is compact by step 5.1; as $x$ and $U$ were arbitrary this is claim 1. [step 2.2, step 3.1, step 4.1, step 4.2, step 5.1]
items/lem-a-locally-compact-hausdorff-space-has-a-base-of-open-sets-with-compact-closure.md:112:7.1 The open subsets of $X$ with compact closure are open, and by step 6.1 every open $U$ and every $x \in U$ admit such a set $V$ with $x \in V \subseteq U$; so by [L7] they form a basis for the topology of $X$, which is claim 2. [step 6.1, L7]
items/lem-a-locally-compact-hausdorff-space-has-a-base-of-open-sets-with-compact-closure.md:114:7.2 Step 6.1 gives, for every $x$ and every open $U \ni x$, an open $V$ with $x \in V \subseteq \overline{V} \subseteq U$, which is condition (b) of [L4] for the space $X$; hence $X$ is regular, which is claim 3. [step 6.1, L4]
items/lem-a-locally-compact-hausdorff-space-has-a-base-of-open-sets-with-compact-closure.md:116:8.1 Steps 6.1, 7.1 and 7.2 are claims 1, 2 and 3, so the lemma is proved. [step 6.1, step 7.1, step 7.2] ∎
items/ex-the-diagonal-in-the-cofinite-and-cocountable-topologies.md:93:1.1 Every singleton of $X$ is finite, hence closed in $\mathcal{T}_{\mathrm{cof}}$, so $(X, \mathcal{T}_{\mathrm{cof}})$ is $T_1$; every singleton of $\mathbb{R}$ is finite, hence at most countable, hence closed in $\mathcal{T}_{\mathrm{coc}}$, so $(\mathbb{R}, \mathcal{T}_{\mathrm{coc}})$ is $T_1$. [A1, A3, L1]
items/ex-the-diagonal-in-the-cofinite-and-cocountable-topologies.md:95:1.2 No two nonempty $U, V \in \mathcal{T}_{\mathrm{cof}}$ are disjoint: $X \setminus U$ and $X \setminus V$ are finite by [A1], so $X \setminus (U \cap V) = (X \setminus U) \cup (X \setminus V)$ is finite by [A3], and $X$ is infinite, so $U \cap V \ne \varnothing$. [A1, A3]
items/ex-the-diagonal-in-the-cofinite-and-cocountable-topologies.md:97:1.3 No two nonempty members of $\mathcal{T}_{\mathrm{coc}}$ on $\mathbb{R}$ are disjoint. [L4]
items/ex-the-diagonal-in-the-cofinite-and-cocountable-topologies.md:99:1.4 Each of $X$ and $\mathbb{R}$ has two distinct points, $X$ being infinite and $\mathbb{R}$ containing $0$ and $1$. [A3]
items/ex-the-diagonal-in-the-cofinite-and-cocountable-topologies.md:101:1.5 Every subset of the countably infinite $Z$ is at most countable by [L5], so every subset of $Z$ has at most countable complement and is therefore open in $\mathcal{T}_{\mathrm{coc}}$; thus $\mathcal{T}_{\mathrm{coc}}$ on $Z$ is the discrete topology. [A1, L5]
items/ex-the-diagonal-in-the-cofinite-and-cocountable-topologies.md:103:2.1 Let $(Y, \mathcal{T})$ be either $(X, \mathcal{T}_{\mathrm{cof}})$ or $(\mathbb{R}, \mathcal{T}_{\mathrm{coc}})$, and let $z \in Y \times Y$ and $U \times W$ be a basic open box containing $z$; then $U \ni z_0$ and $W \ni z_1$ are nonempty open, so $U \cap W \ne \varnothing$ by step 1.2 or step 1.3, and any $t \in U \cap W$ gives $(t,t) \in (U \times W) \cap \Delta_Y$. [step 1.2, step 1.3, A2]
items/ex-the-diagonal-in-the-cofinite-and-cocountable-topologies.md:105:2.2 For distinct $p, q \in Y$ the point $(p,q)$ lies in $Y \times Y$ and not in $\Delta_Y$, so $\Delta_Y \ne Y \times Y$. [step 1.4]
items/ex-the-diagonal-in-the-cofinite-and-cocountable-topologies.md:107:3.1 By step 2.1 and [L2] every point of $Y \times Y$ lies in $\overline{\Delta_Y}$, so $\overline{\Delta_Y} = Y \times Y$, which by step 2.2 differs from $\Delta_Y$; hence $\Delta_Y$ is not closed and by [L3] $Y$ is not Hausdorff. This is claims 1 and 2, together with step 1.1. [step 1.1, step 2.1, step 2.2, L2, L3]
items/ex-the-diagonal-in-the-cofinite-and-cocountable-topologies.md:109:4.1 Distinct $p, q \in Z$ are separated by the disjoint open sets $\{p\}$ and $\{q\}$, so $(Z, \mathcal{T}_{\mathrm{coc}})$ is Hausdorff and by [L3] its diagonal is closed in $Z \times Z$; this is claim 3, and with step 3.1 the example is verified. [step 3.1, step 1.5, L3] ∎
items/thm-a-compact-hausdorff-space-is-regular-and-normal.md:81:1.1 Let $C \subseteq X$ be closed and let $x \in X \setminus C$; since $X$ is compact and $C$ is closed in $X$, the subspace $C$ is compact, and $x$ does not lie in it. [A3, L1]
items/thm-a-compact-hausdorff-space-is-regular-and-normal.md:83:1.2 Let $A, B \subseteq X$ be closed with $A \cap B = \varnothing$; since $X$ is compact and both are closed in $X$, both subspaces $A$ and $B$ are compact. [A3, L1]
items/thm-a-compact-hausdorff-space-is-regular-and-normal.md:85:1.3 $X$ is $T_1$, being Hausdorff. [L3]
items/thm-a-compact-hausdorff-space-is-regular-and-normal.md:87:2.1 By [L2], applied to the point $x$ and the disjoint compact set $C$ of step 1.1, there are disjoint open $U \ni x$ and $V \supseteq C$; as $C$ and $x$ were arbitrary this is exactly the condition of [A1], so $X$ is regular, which is claim 1. [step 1.1, A1, L2]
items/thm-a-compact-hausdorff-space-is-regular-and-normal.md:89:2.2 By [L2], applied to the two disjoint compact sets $A$ and $B$ of step 1.2, there are disjoint open $U \supseteq A$ and $V \supseteq B$; as $A$ and $B$ were arbitrary this is the condition of [A2], so $X$ is normal, which is claim 2. [step 1.2, A2, L2]
items/thm-a-compact-hausdorff-space-is-regular-and-normal.md:91:3.1 By step 1.3 the space is $T_1$; with step 2.1 it is regular and $T_1$, hence $T_3$, and with step 2.2 it is normal and $T_1$, hence $T_4$. This is claim 3. [step 1.3, step 2.1, step 2.2, A1, A2]
items/thm-a-compact-hausdorff-space-is-regular-and-normal.md:93:4.1 Steps 2.1, 2.2 and 3.1 are claims 1, 2 and 3, so a compact Hausdorff space is regular, normal, $T_3$ and $T_4$. [step 2.1, step 2.2, step 3.1] ∎
items/ex-a-finite-hausdorff-space-is-discrete.md:76:1.1 $X$ is $T_1$, being Hausdorff. [L1]
items/ex-a-finite-hausdorff-space-is-discrete.md:78:2.1 Every subset $A \subseteq X$ is finite by [A1], hence closed by step 1.1 and [L2]; so every subset of $X$ is closed. [step 1.1, A1, L2]
items/ex-a-finite-hausdorff-space-is-discrete.md:80:3.1 Every subset $A \subseteq X$ is open, its complement $X \setminus A$ being a subset of $X$ and therefore closed by step 2.1; so $\mathcal{T}$ is the discrete topology, which is claim 1. [step 2.1, A2]
items/ex-a-finite-hausdorff-space-is-discrete.md:82:4.1 Every singleton $\{(u,v)\} = \{u\} \times \{v\}$ of $X \times X$ is a basic open box by step 3.1 and [A3], so every subset of $X \times X$, being the union of the singletons of its elements, is open; hence $X \times X$ is discrete and every subset of it, $\Delta_X$ included, is closed. This is claim 2. [step 3.1, A2, A3] ∎
items/ex-separating-a-point-from-a-finite-set-in-a-hausdorff-space.md:75:1.1 List $F$ as $y_0, \dots, y_{n-1}$ for a natural number $n$, and let $\mathcal{U}$ be a family of sets open in the subspace $F$ whose union is $F$. [A1, A2]
items/ex-separating-a-point-from-a-finite-set-in-a-hausdorff-space.md:77:2.1 For each $i < n$ the set $\mathcal{U}_i := \{\, O \in \mathcal{U} : y_i \in O \,\}$ is nonempty, since the union of $\mathcal{U}$ is $F$ and $y_i \in F$; so by [L1] applied to the function $i \mapsto \mathcal{U}_i$ on $n$ there is a choice function on the family of these sets, and it supplies $O_i \in \mathcal{U}_i$ for every $i < n$. [step 1.1, L1, choose]
items/ex-separating-a-point-from-a-finite-set-in-a-hausdorff-space.md:79:3.1 The finitely many sets $O_0, \dots, O_{n-1}$ lie in $\mathcal{U}$ and their union contains every $y_i$, hence is $F$; as $\mathcal{U}$ was arbitrary, $F$ is compact, which is claim 1. [step 1.1, step 2.1, A2]
items/ex-separating-a-point-from-a-finite-set-in-a-hausdorff-space.md:81:4.1 If $X$ is Hausdorff then, $F$ being compact by step 3.1, [L2] separates $F$ from any point of $X \setminus F$ by disjoint open sets, separates $F$ from any disjoint finite subset of $X$ likewise, and makes $F$ closed in $X$. This is claim 2. [step 3.1, L2] ∎

